<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    This file exists for one setting.

    IIS refuses a request body over 30,000,000 bytes in its own request filtering module, which
    runs natively ahead of the ASP.NET Core Module and therefore ahead of every managed limit this
    application sets. The student and employee register imports carry an Access file — 76 MB for
    1,398 children in the register this was built against — so both of them raise the managed
    limit to a gigabyte with [RequestSizeLimit] / [RequestFormLimits], and under IIS or IIS Express
    both were still answered with a bare "HTTP Error 413.1" page from IIS before a line of
    StudentsController.ImportUpload or EmployeesController.ImportUpload ran. The attributes cannot
    reach this: the request never becomes a managed request.

    So the ceiling is raised here, to the same gigabyte the two upload actions already allow
    (StudentsController.AccessUploadLimit). It is a ceiling, not a licence — every other endpoint
    still stops at the framework default, because the per-action attributes are what actually
    admit a large body, and nothing but those two actions carries one. The upload is streamed
    straight to a temporary file and never held in memory.

    Kestrel does not read this file; it is bound by the same attributes and needs nothing here.
    At publish the SDK adds the aspNetCore handler and element to this file rather than generating
    its own, so the setting travels to a real IIS deployment, which shares the same default.
  -->
  <location path="." inheritInChildApplications="false">
    <system.webServer>
      <security>
        <requestFiltering>
          <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="1073741824" />
        </requestFiltering>
      </security>
    </system.webServer>
  </location>
</configuration>
